In C programming, you can terminate a program using the `return` statement. The `return` statement is used to return a value from a function to the calling function. When `return` is used outside of a function, it is used to terminate the program.

Here's an example of how to terminate a C program using `return`:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    printf("Hello, World!\n");
    return 0;
}
```

In this example, the `return` statement is used to return a value of 0 to the operating system, indicating that the program has completed successfully. If you omit the `return` statement, the program will still terminate, but the operating system will receive an exit code of 0 by default.

Note that the `return` statement should be placed at the end of the `main()` function. If you place it inside a function, it will terminate the execution of that function, but the program will continue running.

Also, it's a good practice to use `return` instead of `exit()` function to terminate

